{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 New York Mets were a team that promised much and delivered chaos. They burst out of the gate with a 2-0 March and a 15-9 April that felt like the foundation of something real — Adrián González crashing a grand slam, Yoenis Céspedes delivering in the clutch, Wilmer Flores providing a walk-off moment, and Jay Bruce adding power. For a brief stretch, the Mets looked like a legitimate contender, and the energy at Citi Field was genuine.\n\nThen May arrived, and with it, a collapse of historic proportions. The Mets went 10-18 in May and then cratered to an unfathomable 5-21 in June — the worst month in recent franchise memory. The bullpen, headlined by costly meltdowns from Jeurys Familia, Paul Sewald, and a parade of overmatched relievers, bled leads and blew games at a stunning rate. Seth Lugo surrendered a two-run blast to Charlie Culberson that still stings. Jacob deGrom, in the middle of his Cy Young campaign, couldn't be bailed out by anyone around him. The roster was depleted by injuries, the front office was in turmoil, and by late June the season felt irretrievably lost.\n\nJuly brought stability — a 12-12 month that felt like catching a breath — and August and September quietly told a more encouraging story. The Mets went 15-16 in August and then surged to 18-10 in September, a scorching finish that was too little, too late but meaningful nonetheless. Brandon Nimmo had become one of the team's best stories, belting clutch home runs and reaching base at an elite clip. Michael Conforto and Todd Frazier delivered a memorable back-to-back shot in September that underscored what this lineup could have been all year.\n\nThe 2018 Mets finished 77-85, out of the playoffs, a record that flattered neither the depths of their June misery nor the genuine promise of their September resurrection. Jacob deGrom won the NL Cy Young Award with historically dominant run prevention, largely wasted by a team that couldn't score or hold leads. It was a season defined by what might have been — a roster with real talent undone by injuries, a shaky bullpen, and one catastrophic two-month stretch that buried any hope before summer was even half over.",
  "chapters": [
